A||N||8||SloPonti||Spark plug question for the 84 fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||SloPonti||04-23-2001||08:21 PM||||what plugs are you using? i am using the stock acdelco plugs now and i was wondering if there is anything better or maybe a hotter burning plug?-just curious what everyone else was using!<BR>thanks<BR>Rob<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/my55ride/fiero6.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Posthumane||04-24-2001||12:09 AM||||I'm running bosch platinums, but i dont' really have anything to compare it to performace wise. Those are the same brand as i got with the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-24-2001||12:38 AM||||For what it worth I used platinum for quit a while and had no complaints maybe a mile or so to the gallon better mpg. Then I went with splitfires now they made quite a difference. I like the way they smoothed out the idle and there was a bit more spunk, they also gave me about 2 to 3 mpg depending on the foot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hank is Here||04-24-2001||01:21 AM||||Where did I put that 10 ft. pole.   Probably better I can't find it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-24-2001||02:05 AM||||I started with Autolites, then switched to Delco Rapidfires.<P>I noticed no difference.  <P>I have also tried Bosch Platinum Plus 4s. My car did not like them . . .<P>1984 Sport Coupe automatic<P>If I were to buy new plugs, I'd just go back to the autolites.  They're cheap and good.||||reg||1
Z||000005||kslish||04-24-2001||08:09 AM||||My coupe with the Duke has AC Rapidfires.  I like them, seems to have a smoother idle (sort of important in the Duke).  A little better fuel economy too.<P>Actually I use Rapidfires in all my GM cars for various reasons (for one, the GT gets them because the plug housings are nickel coated and will not rust in the heads like a basic plain steel plug.  Makes them easier to remove next time around).<P>I just noticed in the stores that they now have Rapidfire Platinum plugs available.  Don't know if they are any better, I use the original Rapidfires and replace them every 30,000 miles.<P>I'm sure there will be a lot of other opinions on plugs, just don't expect any performance miricles from any different brand of plug.<P>Oh yeah, the local exotic car mechanic told me that Bosch + 4's only work well in cars that have extremely turbulent air flow in the cylinders (like a high revving 4 valve per cylinder engine.)  <P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ROB MAC||04-24-2001||11:26 AM||||NGK's work best for me. Don't like paying 3 times as much for platinum and leaving them in 3 times as long.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||04-24-2001||11:34 AM||||I use Bosch Platnums right now. I will be putting in the Delco Rapid fires this year. I have heard that the multi-electrode plugs like splitfires and bosch +4's are engine dependant. Some engines like them and some don't. The only bad thing I heard about the bosch platnum's is they don't like the 87/88 DIS 4-cyl. The reason I am switching to the rapid fires is a friend with an 85 GT used bosch like me and he switched to rapid fires since he is able to get them real cheap (works at a dealer) and he says the car seems to idle better and perform smoother with the larger electrode.||||reg||1
Z||000008||stsmithgt||04-24-2001||11:54 AM||||I'm using Bosch platinum +4's.  At first they were real nice compared to the ones I took out, but they've been in for about a year now, approximately 12,000 miles, and I'm beginning to wonder about there longevity.  My idle is not what it should be and performance could be better.  I think I'm going to try something else though the symptoms could be from other sources.  My engine <I>does</I> have 125,000 miles on it.  I think it needs a geriatric plug. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sprkplug_e0.gif"> I might try splitfire.?¿ Or maybe Cha**___ <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firetruck.gif">  whew*!! that was close!||||reg||1
